<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · 1806 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A lullaby about a little star, written as a poem by Jane Taylor in 1806 and sung to an old French tune.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jane Taylor</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the French melody &quot;Ah! vous dirai-je, maman&quot;, published 1761)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words published 1806; first printed with the tune 1838</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Jane Taylor (1783–1824), published 1806)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twinkle-twinkle-little-star">Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star</a>&quot; is an English lullaby. Its words are a poem, &quot;The Star&quot;, written by Jane Taylor (1783–1824) and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1806 in Rhymes for the Nursery, a collection of poems by Jane and her sister Ann Taylor (1782–1866). The poem is written in couplets and has five stanzas, though only the first is widely known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a> and Chipping Ongar have both claimed to be where Jane wrote it, but Ann Taylor recalled in her autobiography that Jane first saw the name of Ongar, on a signpost in Epping Forest, in the summer of 1810, four years after the poem was published.</p><p>The words were first printed with the French melody &quot;Ah! vous dirai-je, maman&quot; in The Singing Master: First Class Tune Book in 1838, and it is to this tune, first published in 1761 and later arranged by several composers, among them Mozart in his Twelve Variations on &quot;Ah vous dirai-je, Maman&quot;, that the song is sung today; when it is sung, the first two lines of the poem return as a refrain after each stanza. The same melody carries the ABC <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and &quot;Baa, Baa, Black Sheep&quot;. The poem has had many versions and parodies, among them one in Frederick Marryat's novel Poor Jack (1840), another in Mildred J. Hill's Song Stories for the Kindergarten (1896) and &quot;Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Bat&quot;, recited by the Mad Hatter in Lewis Carroll's Alice's Adventures in Wonderland. Its opening words also begin the murder ballad &quot;Duncan and Brady&quot;, and the song can be played as a singing game.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A child looks up at a star shining in the night sky and wonders what it is. The mood is quiet and full of wonder, a song for bedtime.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Twinkle, twinkle, little star,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>How I wonder what you are!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Up above the world so high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Like a diamond in the sky.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When the blazing sun is gone,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When he nothing shines upon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then you show your little light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Twinkle, twinkle, all the night.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then the trav'ller in the dark,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thanks you for your tiny spark,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He could not see which way to go,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If you did not twinkle so.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In the dark blue sky you keep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And often thro' my curtains peep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For you never shut your eye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till the sun is in the sky.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Tis your bright and tiny spark,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lights the trav'ller in the dark:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Tho' I know not what you are,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Twinkle, twinkle, little star.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Jane Taylor, Rhymes for the Nursery (1806). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Rhymes_for_the_Nursery%2FThe_Star" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="KUXhjWOL4EU" data-title="Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star: Jewel, singer (official audio, album Lullaby)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Jewel, singer (official audio, album Lullaby)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Jewel. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KUXhjWOL4EU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Bat</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody recited by the Mad Hatter in Alice's Adventures in Wonderland</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Poor Jack (Frederick Marryat)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a version in the novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song Stories for the Kindergarten (Mildred J. Hill)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1896</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Duncan and Brady</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its opening words begin the murder ballad</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ah! vous dirai-je, maman</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1761</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its melody</span></li></ul></section>
